var tipuesearch = {"pages":[{"title":" Fortran Rationals ","text":"Fortran Rationals Fortran rationals About A package that implements rational numbers as a numeric type, for exact computations. Arithmetic procedures are all elemental. Values are kept in lowest terms with a positive denominator. Arithmetic is checked: an operation whose exact result does not fit in 64 bits stops with an error instead of returning a wrong answer. Install Add the package to the [dependencies] of your fpm.toml : [dependencies] fortran_rationals = { git = \"https://github.com/willklausler/fortran_rationals\" } Build With fpm : fpm build\nfpm test fpm run --example Without fpm, the library is a single file: gfortran -c src/rationals.f90 (or any Fortran 2018 compiler). Use Here is an overview; see the test and example code for all available uses. use rationals ! Declare type type ( rational ) :: a , b , c ! Set value via constructor a = rational ( 2 , 3 ) ! fraction 2/3 b = rational ( 2 ) ! integer 2 c = rational ( \"3/4\" ) ! fraction 3/4 c = rational ( 0.75d0 ) ! fraction 3/4, the simplest fraction equal to the real ! Set value via assignment a = 2 ! integer 2 ! Write a value write ( * , * ) rational ( 4 , 5 ) ! Check if rational is an integer write ( * , \"(L1)\" ) a % is_integer () ! Do conditional and arithmetic if ( rational ( 5 , 8 ) < rational ( 8 , 13 )) then write ( * , * ) rational ( 8 , 13 ) - rational ( 5 , 8 ) else write ( * , * ) ( rational ( 8 , 13 ) + rational ( 5 , 8 )) / 21 end if The module does not export kind parameters. Where a kind is needed, use int32 , int64 and real64 from iso_fortran_env . Limitations Range. Numerator and denominator are int64 . Repeated sums and products grow denominators quickly, so long computations (for example Gaussian elimination on larger matrices) can exceed this range. The program then stops with rational: integer overflow . There is no arbitrary-precision fallback. Errors stop the program. Overflow, division by zero, and unparsable text all use error stop , so they cannot be caught. Integer kinds. Mixed rational-integer operators accept int32 integers. Construct int64 values with rational(n, d) . Reals. Conversion from real64 gives the simplest fraction that converts back to exactly the same real. A decimal like 0.1 becomes 1/10 , but this is the value the real represents, not necessarily the one intended. Formatted input. read with DT or list-directed formatting works for values in the middle of a record, but compilers differ at the end of a record: gfortran skips the next record, ifx reports end-of-record for (DT) , and nvfortran fails. For portable input, read a line into a character variable and use rational(str) . References D. E. Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming , Vol. 2: Seminumerical Algorithms , 3rd ed., Addison-Wesley, 1997, Section 4.5.1, “Fractions”. Addition and multiplication follow its reduce-before-multiply method. License MIT Developer Info Will Klausler","tags":"home","url":"index.html"},{"title":"rational – Fortran Rationals ","text":"type, public :: rational Rational number num/den in lowest terms with den > 0 Constructor public        interface rational Construct from numerator and denominator, an integer, a real,\nor text such as “3/4”, “-2” or “0.75” private elemental function rat_const_real(r) result(rat) Construct rational from real: the simplest fraction that converts\nback to exactly r, or else the closest continued-fraction\nconvergent that fits in 64 bits Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=rk), intent(in) :: r Return Value type( rational ) private impure elemental function rat_const_char(str) result(r) Construct rational from character, such as “3/4”, “-2” or “0.75” Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: str Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_const_int(n) result(rat) Construct rational from integer Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: n Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_const_ik(n, d) result(rat) Construct rational from numerator and denominator Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: n Numerator integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: d Denominator Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_const_ir(n, d) result(rat) Construct rational from numerator and denominator Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: n Numerator integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: d Denominator Return Value type( rational ) Type-Bound Procedures procedure, public :: get_den private elemental function get_den(self) result(den) Get rational denominator Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( rational ), intent(in) :: self Return Value integer(kind=ir) procedure, public :: get_num private elemental function get_num(self) result(num) Get rational numerator Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( rational ), intent(in) :: self Return Value integer(kind=ir) procedure, public :: inverse private elemental function inverse(self) result(b) Reciprocal of rational; stops if it is zero Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( rational ), intent(in) :: self Return Value type( rational ) procedure, public :: is_integer private elemental function is_integer(self) result(res) Check if rational is integer Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( rational ), intent(in) :: self Return Value logical generic, public :: read(formatted) => read_formatted private  subroutine read_formatted(self, unit, iotype, v_list, iostat, iomsg) Read formatted rational: one token delimited by blanks, a comma,\nor the end of the record Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( rational ), intent(inout) :: self integer, intent(in) :: unit character(len=*), intent(in) :: iotype integer, intent(in) :: v_list (:) integer, intent(out) :: iostat character(len=*), intent(inout) :: iomsg procedure, public :: read_formatted private  subroutine read_formatted(self, unit, iotype, v_list, iostat, iomsg) Read formatted rational: one token delimited by blanks, a comma,\nor the end of the record Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( rational ), intent(inout) :: self integer, intent(in) :: unit character(len=*), intent(in) :: iotype integer, intent(in) :: v_list (:) integer, intent(out) :: iostat character(len=*), intent(inout) :: iomsg procedure, public :: simplify private elemental subroutine simplify(a) Reduce to lowest terms with a positive denominator Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( rational ), intent(inout) :: a generic, public :: write(formatted) => write_formatted private  subroutine write_formatted(self, unit, iotype, v_list, iostat, iomsg) Write formatted rational, in the same form as char() Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( rational ), intent(in) :: self integer, intent(in) :: unit character(len=*), intent(in) :: iotype integer, intent(in) :: v_list (:) integer, intent(out) :: iostat character(len=*), intent(inout) :: iomsg procedure, public :: write_formatted private  subroutine write_formatted(self, unit, iotype, v_list, iostat, iomsg) Write formatted rational, in the same form as char() Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( rational ), intent(in) :: self integer, intent(in) :: unit character(len=*), intent(in) :: iotype integer, intent(in) :: v_list (:) integer, intent(out) :: iostat character(len=*), intent(inout) :: iomsg Source Code type :: rational !! Rational number num/den in lowest terms with den > 0 private integer ( ir ) :: num = 0 !! Numerator integer ( ir ) :: den = 1 !! Denominator contains procedure :: simplify procedure :: get_num , get_den procedure :: is_integer procedure :: inverse procedure :: write_formatted generic :: write ( formatted ) => write_formatted procedure :: read_formatted generic :: read ( formatted ) => read_formatted end type rational","tags":"","url":"type/rational.html"},{"title":"set_order – Fortran Rationals","text":"public  subroutine set_order(ord) Set the denominator used by random_number Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: ord Source Code subroutine set_order ( ord ) !! Set the denominator used by random_number integer ( ik ), intent ( in ) :: ord order = ord end subroutine set_order","tags":"","url":"proc/set_order.html"},{"title":"abs – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface abs Absolute value Module Procedures private elemental function rat_abs(b) result(a) Absolute value Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/abs.html"},{"title":"assignment(=) – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface assignment(=) Assign a rational, integer, or real to a rational Module Procedures private elemental subroutine rat_set_rat(self, b) Set rational = rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(out) :: self type( rational ), intent(in) :: b private elemental subroutine rat_set_int(self, b) Set rational = Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(out) :: self integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b private elemental subroutine rat_set_real(self, b) Set rational = Real Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(out) :: self real(kind=rk), intent(in) :: b","tags":"","url":"interface/assignment(=).html"},{"title":"ceiling – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface ceiling Smallest integer >= argument Module Procedures private elemental function rat_ceil(b) result(n) Smallest integer >= b Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ir)","tags":"","url":"interface/ceiling.html"},{"title":"char – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface char Text form: “n/d”, or “n” for an integer Module Procedures private elemental function rat_str(self) result(str) Convert rational to character Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( rational ), intent(in) :: self Return Value character(len=rat_len)","tags":"","url":"interface/char.html"},{"title":"compare – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface compare Three-way comparison: -1, 0 or 1 Module Procedures private elemental function rat_compare(a, b) result(c) Three-way comparison: -1 if a b Compares continued-fraction expansions, so it never overflows. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer","tags":"","url":"interface/compare.html"},{"title":"divmod – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface divmod Truncated quotient and remainder together Module Procedures private elemental subroutine rat_divmod(a, b, q, r) Compute integer quotient and rational remainder of a/b simultaneously Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b integer(kind=ir), intent(out) :: q truncated quotient type( rational ), intent(out) :: r remainder: a - q*b","tags":"","url":"interface/divmod.html"},{"title":"dot_product – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface dot_product Dot product of rational vectors Module Procedures private pure function rat_dot(a, b) result(c) Dot product of two rational vectors Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a (:) type( rational ), intent(in) :: b (:) Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/dot_product.html"},{"title":"floor – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface floor Greatest integer <= argument Module Procedures private elemental function rat_floor(b) result(n) Greatest integer <= b Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ir)","tags":"","url":"interface/floor.html"},{"title":"gcf – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface gcf Greatest common factor Module Procedures private elemental function gcf_ir(a, b) result(c) Calculate the greatest common factor of two integers Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ir) private elemental function gcf_ik(a, b) result(c) Calculate the greatest common factor of two int32 integers Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ik) private elemental function gcf_rat(a, b) result(c) Calculate the greatest common factor of two rationals Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/gcf.html"},{"title":"int – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface int Integer part, truncated toward zero like the intrinsic Module Procedures private elemental function rat_int(b) result(i) Convert rational to integer, truncating toward zero Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ik)","tags":"","url":"interface/int.html"},{"title":"lcm – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface lcm Least common multiple Module Procedures private elemental function lcm_ir(a, b) result(c) Calculate the least common multiple of two integers Divide before multiplying to avoid intermediate overflow Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ir) private elemental function lcm_ik(a, b) result(c) Calculate the least common multiple of two int32 integers Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ik)","tags":"","url":"interface/lcm.html"},{"title":"matmul – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface matmul Matrix-matrix and matrix-vector product Module Procedures private pure function rat_matmul(a, b) result(c) Matrix multiply two rational arrays Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a (:,:) type( rational ), intent(in) :: b (:,:) Return Value type( rational ), (size(a,1),size(b,2)) private pure function rat_matvec(a, b) result(c) Matrix multiply a rational matrix with a rational vector Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a (:,:) type( rational ), intent(in) :: b (:) Return Value type( rational ), (size(a,1))","tags":"","url":"interface/matmul.html"},{"title":"max – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface max Larger of two rationals Module Procedures private elemental function rat_max(a, b) result(c) Maximum rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/max.html"},{"title":"min – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface min Smaller of two rationals Module Procedures private elemental function rat_min(a, b) result(c) Minimum rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/min.html"},{"title":"mod – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface mod Remainder a - trunc(a/b)*b Module Procedures private elemental function rat_mod(a, b) result(c) Rational modulo: a - trunc(a/b)*b Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/mod.html"},{"title":"operator(*) – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface operator(*) Multiplication Module Procedures private elemental function rat_mul_rat(a, b) result(c) Multiply two rationals, cancelling cross factors first\n(Knuth, TAOCP Vol. 2, 4.5.1) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_mul_int(a, b) result(c) Multiply rational and integer Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function int_mul_rat(a, b) result(c) Multiply integer and rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/operator(ASTERISK).html"},{"title":"operator(**) – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface operator(**) Integer power Module Procedures private elemental function rat_power_int(a, b) result(c) Raise rational to integer power Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/operator(ASTERISKASTERISK).html"},{"title":"operator(+) – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface operator(+) Addition, and unary plus Module Procedures private elemental function rat_add_rat(a, b) result(c) Add two rationals (Knuth, TAOCP Vol. 2, 4.5.1); the result is\nalready in lowest terms Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_add_int(a, b) result(c) Add rational and integer Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function int_add_rat(a, b) result(c) Add integer and rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function unary_plus(self) result(r) Unary plus: returns its argument Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: self Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/operator(+).html"},{"title":"operator(-) – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface operator(-) Subtraction, and negation Module Procedures private elemental function rat_sub_rat(a, b) result(c) Subtract two rationals Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_sub_int(a, b) result(c) Subtract integer from rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function int_sub_rat(a, b) result(c) Subtract rational from integer Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function negate(b) result(n) Negate rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/operator(-).html"},{"title":"operator(/) – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface operator(/) Division; stops on division by zero Module Procedures private elemental function rat_div_rat(a, b) result(c) Divide two rationals Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_div_int(a, b) result(c) Divide rational by integer Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function int_div_rat(a, b) result(c) Divide integer by rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/operator(SLASH).html"},{"title":"operator(/=) – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface operator(/=) Inequality Module Procedures private elemental function rat_ne_rat(a, b) result(c) Check rational != rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function rat_ne_int(a, b) result(c) Check rational != Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function int_ne_rat(a, b) result(c) Check Int /= rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical","tags":"","url":"interface/operator(SLASH=).html"},{"title":"operator(<) – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface operator(<) Less than Module Procedures private elemental function rat_lt_rat(a, b) result(c) Check rational < rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function rat_lt_int(a, b) result(c) Check rational < Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function int_lt_rat(a, b) result(c) Check Int < rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical","tags":"","url":"interface/operator(lt).html"},{"title":"operator(<=) – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface operator(<=) Less than or equal Module Procedures private elemental function rat_le_rat(a, b) result(c) Check rational <= rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function rat_le_int(a, b) result(c) Check rational <= Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function int_le_rat(a, b) result(c) Check Int <= rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical","tags":"","url":"interface/operator(lt=).html"},{"title":"operator(==) – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface operator(==) Equality Module Procedures private elemental function rat_eq_rat(a, b) result(c) Check rational == rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function rat_eq_int(a, b) result(c) Check rational == Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function int_eq_rat(a, b) result(c) Check Int == rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical","tags":"","url":"interface/operator(==).html"},{"title":"operator(>) – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface operator(>) Greater than Module Procedures private elemental function rat_gt_rat(a, b) result(c) Check rational > rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function rat_gt_int(a, b) result(c) Check rational > Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function int_gt_rat(a, b) result(c) Check Int > rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical","tags":"","url":"interface/operator(gt).html"},{"title":"operator(>=) – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface operator(>=) Greater than or equal Module Procedures private elemental function rat_ge_rat(a, b) result(c) Check rational >= rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function rat_ge_int(a, b) result(c) Check rational >= Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function int_ge_rat(a, b) result(c) Check Int >= rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical","tags":"","url":"interface/operator(gt=).html"},{"title":"product – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface product Product of a rational vector Module Procedures private pure function rat_product(a) result(p) Product of a rational array Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a (:) Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/product.html"},{"title":"random_number – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface random_number Random rational in [0, 1] with denominator dividing the order\nset by set_order Module Procedures private impure elemental subroutine rat_rand(a) Random rational in [0, 1] with denominator dividing order Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(out) :: a","tags":"","url":"interface/random_number.html"},{"title":"rational – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface rational Construct from numerator and denominator, an integer, a real,\nor text such as “3/4”, “-2” or “0.75” Module Procedures private elemental function rat_const_real(r) result(rat) Construct rational from real: the simplest fraction that converts\nback to exactly r, or else the closest continued-fraction\nconvergent that fits in 64 bits Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=rk), intent(in) :: r Return Value type( rational ) private impure elemental function rat_const_char(str) result(r) Construct rational from character, such as “3/4”, “-2” or “0.75” Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: str Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_const_int(n) result(rat) Construct rational from integer Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: n Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_const_ik(n, d) result(rat) Construct rational from numerator and denominator Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: n Numerator integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: d Denominator Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_const_ir(n, d) result(rat) Construct rational from numerator and denominator Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: n Numerator integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: d Denominator Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/rational.html"},{"title":"real – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface real Nearest real(real64) Module Procedures private elemental function rat_real(b) result(r) Convert rational to real Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value real(kind=rk)","tags":"","url":"interface/real.html"},{"title":"round – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface round Nearest integer, ties away from zero Module Procedures private elemental function rat_round(b) result(n) Round to nearest integer; ties round away from zero r >= den - r is 2r >= den without overflow Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ir)","tags":"","url":"interface/round.html"},{"title":"sign – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface sign Magnitude of the first argument with the sign of the second Module Procedures private elemental function rat_sign(a, b) result(c) Sign transfer: magnitude of a with sign of b (mirrors intrinsic sign()) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/sign.html"},{"title":"sum – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface sum Sum of a rational vector Module Procedures private pure function rat_sum(a) result(s) Sum of a rational array Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a (:) Return Value type( rational )","tags":"","url":"interface/sum.html"},{"title":"transpose – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface transpose Matrix transpose Module Procedures private pure function rat_transpose(a) result(b) Transpose rational matrix Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a (:,:) Return Value type( rational ), (size(a,2),size(a,1))","tags":"","url":"interface/transpose.html"},{"title":"trunc – Fortran Rationals","text":"public interface trunc Integer part, truncated toward zero Module Procedures private elemental function rat_trunc(b) result(n) Truncation toward zero (same as int() for positive, ceiling for negative)\nFortran integer division truncates toward zero Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ir)","tags":"","url":"interface/trunc.html"},{"title":"ex1 – Fortran Rationals","text":"subroutine ex1() Rational representation of pi, and its decimal truncations Arguments None Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public :: i real(kind=rk), public, parameter :: pi = 4*atan(1.0_rk) type( rational ), public :: rat_pi Source Code subroutine ex1 () !! Rational representation of pi, and its decimal truncations integer :: i real ( rk ), parameter :: pi = 4 * atan ( 1.0_rk ) type ( rational ) :: rat_pi write ( * , \"(A)\" ) \"Example: rational representation of pi\" rat_pi = pi write ( * , * ) pi write ( * , * ) rat_pi do i = 1 , 8 write ( * , * ) rational ( int ( pi * 10 ** i ), 10 ** i ) end do ! i write ( * , * ) end subroutine ex1","tags":"","url":"proc/ex1.html"},{"title":"ex2 – Fortran Rationals","text":"subroutine ex2() Continued-fraction iteration converging to the square root of two Arguments None Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public :: i type( rational ), public :: roottwo Source Code subroutine ex2 () !! Continued-fraction iteration converging to the square root of two integer :: i type ( rational ) :: roottwo write ( * , \"(A)\" ) \"Example: approximation of the square root of two\" roottwo = 1 do i = 1 , 10 roottwo = 1 + 1 / ( 1 + roottwo ) write ( * , \"(I3,')',1x)\" , advance = 'no' ) i write ( * , * ) roottwo , real ( roottwo ), real ( roottwo * roottwo ) end do ! i end subroutine ex2","tags":"","url":"proc/ex2.html"},{"title":"rationals – Fortran Rationals","text":"Rational numbers as a derived type with exact arithmetic. A rational holds a 64-bit numerator and denominator, always in lowest\n terms with a positive denominator. Arithmetic is checked: an operation\n whose exact result does not fit in 64 bits stops with an error instead of\n returning a wrong value. Division by zero also stops with an error. Addition and multiplication reduce by common factors before multiplying,\n following D. E. Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming , Vol. 2:\n Seminumerical Algorithms, 3rd ed., Addison-Wesley, 1997, Section 4.5.1. * * * I / O * * * * * * * * * C O N S T R U C T O R S * * * *** * * * C A S T I N G * * * * * * * * * A S S I G N M E N T * * * * * * * C H E C K E D  I N T E G E R S * * *** * * * A D D I T I O N * * * * **** * * * S U B T R A C T I O N * * * * * * * M U L T I P L I C A T I O N * * * * * * * D I V I S I O N * * * * **** * * * E X P O N E N T I A T I O N * * * * * * * E Q U A L I T Y * * * * **** * * * I N E Q U A L I T Y * * * * * * * S U P E R I O R I T Y * * * * * * * I N F E R I O R I T Y * * * * * * * S U P E R I O R I T Y * * * * * * * I N F E R I O R I T Y * * * * * * * U T I L I T Y * * * * * * * * * I N T R I N S I C  F U N C T I O N S * **** * * * F U N C T I O N S * * * * ** * * * E N D * * * * * * Uses iso_fortran_env Interfaces public        interface abs Absolute value private elemental function rat_abs(b) result(a) Absolute value Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) public        interface assignment(=) Assign a rational, integer, or real to a rational private elemental subroutine rat_set_rat(self, b) Set rational = rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(out) :: self type( rational ), intent(in) :: b private elemental subroutine rat_set_int(self, b) Set rational = Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(out) :: self integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b private elemental subroutine rat_set_real(self, b) Set rational = Real Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(out) :: self real(kind=rk), intent(in) :: b public        interface ceiling Smallest integer >= argument private elemental function rat_ceil(b) result(n) Smallest integer >= b Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ir) public        interface char Text form: “n/d”, or “n” for an integer private elemental function rat_str(self) result(str) Convert rational to character Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( rational ), intent(in) :: self Return Value character(len=rat_len) public        interface compare Three-way comparison: -1, 0 or 1 private elemental function rat_compare(a, b) result(c) Three-way comparison: -1 if a b Compares continued-fraction expansions, so it never overflows. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer public        interface divmod Truncated quotient and remainder together private elemental subroutine rat_divmod(a, b, q, r) Compute integer quotient and rational remainder of a/b simultaneously Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b integer(kind=ir), intent(out) :: q truncated quotient type( rational ), intent(out) :: r remainder: a - q*b public        interface dot_product Dot product of rational vectors private pure function rat_dot(a, b) result(c) Dot product of two rational vectors Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a (:) type( rational ), intent(in) :: b (:) Return Value type( rational ) public        interface floor Greatest integer <= argument private elemental function rat_floor(b) result(n) Greatest integer <= b Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ir) public        interface gcf Greatest common factor private elemental function gcf_ir(a, b) result(c) Calculate the greatest common factor of two integers Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ir) private elemental function gcf_ik(a, b) result(c) Calculate the greatest common factor of two int32 integers Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ik) private elemental function gcf_rat(a, b) result(c) Calculate the greatest common factor of two rationals Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) public        interface int Integer part, truncated toward zero like the intrinsic private elemental function rat_int(b) result(i) Convert rational to integer, truncating toward zero Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ik) public        interface lcm Least common multiple private elemental function lcm_ir(a, b) result(c) Calculate the least common multiple of two integers Divide before multiplying to avoid intermediate overflow Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ir) private elemental function lcm_ik(a, b) result(c) Calculate the least common multiple of two int32 integers Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ik) public        interface matmul Matrix-matrix and matrix-vector product private pure function rat_matmul(a, b) result(c) Matrix multiply two rational arrays Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a (:,:) type( rational ), intent(in) :: b (:,:) Return Value type( rational ), (size(a,1),size(b,2)) private pure function rat_matvec(a, b) result(c) Matrix multiply a rational matrix with a rational vector Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a (:,:) type( rational ), intent(in) :: b (:) Return Value type( rational ), (size(a,1)) public        interface max Larger of two rationals private elemental function rat_max(a, b) result(c) Maximum rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) public        interface min Smaller of two rationals private elemental function rat_min(a, b) result(c) Minimum rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) public        interface mod Remainder a - trunc(a/b)*b private elemental function rat_mod(a, b) result(c) Rational modulo: a - trunc(a/b)*b Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) public        interface operator(*) Multiplication private elemental function rat_mul_rat(a, b) result(c) Multiply two rationals, cancelling cross factors first\n(Knuth, TAOCP Vol. 2, 4.5.1) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_mul_int(a, b) result(c) Multiply rational and integer Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function int_mul_rat(a, b) result(c) Multiply integer and rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) public        interface operator(**) Integer power private elemental function rat_power_int(a, b) result(c) Raise rational to integer power Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) public        interface operator(+) Addition, and unary plus private elemental function rat_add_rat(a, b) result(c) Add two rationals (Knuth, TAOCP Vol. 2, 4.5.1); the result is\nalready in lowest terms Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_add_int(a, b) result(c) Add rational and integer Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function int_add_rat(a, b) result(c) Add integer and rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function unary_plus(self) result(r) Unary plus: returns its argument Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: self Return Value type( rational ) public        interface operator(-) Subtraction, and negation private elemental function rat_sub_rat(a, b) result(c) Subtract two rationals Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_sub_int(a, b) result(c) Subtract integer from rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function int_sub_rat(a, b) result(c) Subtract rational from integer Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function negate(b) result(n) Negate rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) public        interface operator(/) Division; stops on division by zero private elemental function rat_div_rat(a, b) result(c) Divide two rationals Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_div_int(a, b) result(c) Divide rational by integer Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function int_div_rat(a, b) result(c) Divide integer by rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) public        interface operator(/=) Inequality private elemental function rat_ne_rat(a, b) result(c) Check rational != rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function rat_ne_int(a, b) result(c) Check rational != Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function int_ne_rat(a, b) result(c) Check Int /= rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical public        interface operator(<) Less than private elemental function rat_lt_rat(a, b) result(c) Check rational < rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function rat_lt_int(a, b) result(c) Check rational < Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function int_lt_rat(a, b) result(c) Check Int < rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical public        interface operator(<=) Less than or equal private elemental function rat_le_rat(a, b) result(c) Check rational <= rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function rat_le_int(a, b) result(c) Check rational <= Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function int_le_rat(a, b) result(c) Check Int <= rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical public        interface operator(==) Equality private elemental function rat_eq_rat(a, b) result(c) Check rational == rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function rat_eq_int(a, b) result(c) Check rational == Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function int_eq_rat(a, b) result(c) Check Int == rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical public        interface operator(>) Greater than private elemental function rat_gt_rat(a, b) result(c) Check rational > rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function rat_gt_int(a, b) result(c) Check rational > Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function int_gt_rat(a, b) result(c) Check Int > rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical public        interface operator(>=) Greater than or equal private elemental function rat_ge_rat(a, b) result(c) Check rational >= rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function rat_ge_int(a, b) result(c) Check rational >= Int Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical private elemental function int_ge_rat(a, b) result(c) Check Int >= rational Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value logical public        interface product Product of a rational vector private pure function rat_product(a) result(p) Product of a rational array Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a (:) Return Value type( rational ) public        interface random_number Random rational in [0, 1] with denominator dividing the order\nset by set_order private impure elemental subroutine rat_rand(a) Random rational in [0, 1] with denominator dividing order Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(out) :: a public        interface rational Construct from numerator and denominator, an integer, a real,\nor text such as “3/4”, “-2” or “0.75” private elemental function rat_const_real(r) result(rat) Construct rational from real: the simplest fraction that converts\nback to exactly r, or else the closest continued-fraction\nconvergent that fits in 64 bits Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=rk), intent(in) :: r Return Value type( rational ) private impure elemental function rat_const_char(str) result(r) Construct rational from character, such as “3/4”, “-2” or “0.75” Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: str Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_const_int(n) result(rat) Construct rational from integer Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: n Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_const_ik(n, d) result(rat) Construct rational from numerator and denominator Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: n Numerator integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: d Denominator Return Value type( rational ) private elemental function rat_const_ir(n, d) result(rat) Construct rational from numerator and denominator Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: n Numerator integer(kind=ir), intent(in) :: d Denominator Return Value type( rational ) public        interface real Nearest real(real64) private elemental function rat_real(b) result(r) Convert rational to real Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value real(kind=rk) public        interface round Nearest integer, ties away from zero private elemental function rat_round(b) result(n) Round to nearest integer; ties round away from zero r >= den - r is 2r >= den without overflow Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ir) public        interface sign Magnitude of the first argument with the sign of the second private elemental function rat_sign(a, b) result(c) Sign transfer: magnitude of a with sign of b (mirrors intrinsic sign()) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value type( rational ) public        interface sum Sum of a rational vector private pure function rat_sum(a) result(s) Sum of a rational array Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a (:) Return Value type( rational ) public        interface transpose Matrix transpose private pure function rat_transpose(a) result(b) Transpose rational matrix Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: a (:,:) Return Value type( rational ), (size(a,2),size(a,1)) public        interface trunc Integer part, truncated toward zero private elemental function rat_trunc(b) result(n) Truncation toward zero (same as int() for positive, ceiling for negative)\nFortran integer division truncates toward zero Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( rational ), intent(in) :: b Return Value integer(kind=ir) Derived Types type, public :: rational Rational number num/den in lowest terms with den > 0 Constructor Construct from numerator and denominator, an integer, a real,\nor text such as “3/4”, “-2” or “0.75” private\n\n                    elemental\n                    function rat_const_real (r) Construct rational from real: the simplest fraction that converts\nback to exactly r, or else the closest continued-fraction\nconvergent that fits in 64 bits private\n\n                    impure, elemental\n                    function rat_const_char (str) Construct rational from character, such as “3/4”, “-2” or “0.75” private\n\n                    elemental\n                    function rat_const_int (n) Construct rational from integer private\n\n                    elemental\n                    function rat_const_ik (n, d) Construct rational from numerator and denominator private\n\n                    elemental\n                    function rat_const_ir (n, d) Construct rational from numerator and denominator Type-Bound Procedures procedure, public :: get_den procedure, public :: get_num procedure, public :: inverse procedure, public :: is_integer generic, public :: read(formatted) => read_formatted procedure, public :: read_formatted procedure, public :: simplify generic, public :: write(formatted) => write_formatted procedure, public :: write_formatted Subroutines public  subroutine set_order (ord) Set the denominator used by random_number Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=ik), intent(in) :: ord","tags":"","url":"module/rationals.html"},{"title":"example – Fortran Rationals","text":"Uses iso_fortran_env rationals Rational approximations of pi and the square root of two Subroutines subroutine ex1 () Rational representation of pi, and its decimal truncations Arguments None subroutine ex2 () Continued-fraction iteration converging to the square root of two Arguments None Source Code program example !! Rational approximations of pi and the square root of two use rationals use iso_fortran_env , only : rk => real64 implicit none call ex1 () call ex2 () contains subroutine ex1 () !! Rational representation of pi, and its decimal truncations integer :: i real ( rk ), parameter :: pi = 4 * atan ( 1.0_rk ) type ( rational ) :: rat_pi write ( * , \"(A)\" ) \"Example: rational representation of pi\" rat_pi = pi write ( * , * ) pi write ( * , * ) rat_pi do i = 1 , 8 write ( * , * ) rational ( int ( pi * 10 ** i ), 10 ** i ) end do ! i write ( * , * ) end subroutine ex1 !*********************************************************************** subroutine ex2 () !! Continued-fraction iteration converging to the square root of two integer :: i type ( rational ) :: roottwo write ( * , \"(A)\" ) \"Example: approximation of the square root of two\" roottwo = 1 do i = 1 , 10 roottwo = 1 + 1 / ( 1 + roottwo ) write ( * , \"(I3,')',1x)\" , advance = 'no' ) i write ( * , * ) roottwo , real ( roottwo ), real ( roottwo * roottwo ) end do ! i end subroutine ex2 end program example","tags":"","url":"program/example.html"},{"title":"rationals_test – Fortran Rationals","text":"Uses iso_fortran_env rationals Showcase rationals write(DT) emits char(); formatted DT input is compiler dependent\nSign transfer: magnitude of a, sign of b\nZero as b: treat as non-negative (matches intrinsic convention)\nSame value, different representation (relies on simplify invariant) Positive dividend and divisor\n7/3 mod 1/2: trunc(7/3 / (1/2)) = trunc(14/3) = 4; rem = 7/3 - 4 (1/2) = 7/3 - 2 = 1/3\nExact multiple: remainder is zero\nNegative dividend: remainder has same sign as dividend (matches Fortran mod)\ntrunc(-14/3) = -4; rem = -7/3 - (-4) (1/2) = -7/3 + 2 = -1/3 Expected: q = trunc(14/3) = 4, r = 1/3\nReconstruction: b*q + r == a\nExact division: remainder zero, quotient exact Known result: 1/2 + 2/3 + 1/6 = 3/6 + 4/6 + 1/6 = 8/6 = 4/3\nSingle element: sum == element\nSum of empty array should be zero (additive identity) Known result: 1/2 * 2/3 * 3/4 = 6/24 = 1/4\nSingle element\nProduct containing zero: result is zero\nEmpty array should be one (multiplicative identity) Real conversion gives the simplest exact fraction\nReciprocal of a negative keeps the denominator positive\nint() truncates like the intrinsic\nComparison near the int64 limit must not overflow\nAddition cancels common denominator factors before multiplying\nNon-square matrix-vector product Variables Type Attributes Name Initial type( rational ) :: a type( rational ) :: b character(len=*), parameter :: fmt1 = \"(A20,': ')\" character(len=*), parameter :: fmt2 = \"('passed')\" type( rational ) :: m (2,2) type( rational ) :: n (2,2) integer(kind=ik) :: ord type( rational ) :: p (2,2) character(len=50) :: str type( rational ) :: u (2) type( rational ) :: v (2) integer(kind=ir), parameter :: yoog = huge(1_ir) Source Code program rationals_test !! Showcase rationals use rationals use iso_fortran_env , only : ir => int64 , ik => int32 , rk => real64 implicit none integer ( ir ), parameter :: yoog = huge ( 1_ir ) integer ( ik ) :: ord character ( * ), parameter :: fmt1 = \"(A20,': ')\" character ( * ), parameter :: fmt2 = \"('passed')\" character ( 50 ) :: str type ( rational ) :: a , b type ( rational ) :: u ( 2 ), v ( 2 ), m ( 2 , 2 ), n ( 2 , 2 ), p ( 2 , 2 ) str = \"1/2  \" write ( * , \"(A)\" ) \"--- Testing rationals ---\" write ( * , \"(A)\" ) \"Basics\" write ( * , fmt1 , advance = 'no' ) \"Math\" if ( gcf ( 0 , 2 ) /= 2 ) then error stop \"gcf failed for 0\" end if if ( gcf ( 2 , 3 ) /= 1 ) then error stop \"gcf failed for coprime\" end if if ( gcf ( 12 , 12 ) /= 12 ) then error stop \"gcf failed for equality\" end if if ( gcf ( 3 , 12 ) /= 3 ) then error stop \"gcf failed for result\" end if if ( gcf ( - 3 , 12 ) /= 3 ) then error stop \"gcf failed for negative input\" end if if ( lcm ( 0 , 1 ) /= 0 ) then error stop \"lcm failed for zero\" end if if ( lcm ( 3 , 3 ) /= 3 ) then error stop \"lcm failed for equality\" end if if ( lcm ( 2 , 3 ) /= 6 ) then error stop \"lcm failed for product result\" end if if ( lcm ( 4 , 6 ) /= 12 ) then error stop \"lcm failed for non-product result\" end if if ( lcm ( - 4 , 6 ) /= 12 ) then error stop \"lcm failed for negative\" end if write ( * , fmt2 ) write ( * , fmt1 , advance = 'no' ) \"Constructor\" a = rational ( 1 , 1 ) if (( a % get_num () /= 1 ) . or . ( a % get_den () /= 1 )) then error stop \"Default constructor failed\" end if a = rational ( 3 ) if (( a % get_num () /= 3 ) . or . ( a % get_den () /= 1 )) then error stop \"Integer constructor failed\" end if a = rational ( 0.5_rk ) if (( a % get_num () /= 1 ) . or . ( a % get_den () /= 2 )) then error stop \"Real constructor failed\" end if a = rational ( \"1/2\" ) if (( a % get_num () /= 1 ) . or . ( a % get_den () /= 2 )) then error stop \"Character constructor failed\" end if write ( * , fmt2 ) write ( * , fmt1 , advance = 'no' ) \"Text round-trip\" !! write(DT) emits char(); formatted DT input is compiler dependent a = rational ( - 7_ik , 12_ik ) if ( rational ( trim ( char ( a ))) /= a ) then error stop \"Text round-trip: value changed through char/parse\" end if if ( char ( rational ( - 3_ik )) /= \"-3\" ) then error stop \"char failed for integer\" end if write ( * , fmt2 ) write ( * , fmt1 , advance = 'no' ) \"Assignment\" a = 2 if (( a % get_num () /= 2 ) . or . ( a % get_den () /= 1 )) then error stop \"Integer assignment failed\" end if a = 0.5_rk if (( a % get_num () /= 1 ) . or . ( a % get_den () /= 2 )) then error stop \"Real assignment failed\" end if ! a = \"1/2\"   !! Not supported by Fortran b = a if (( b % get_num () /= a % get_num ()) . or . ( b % get_den () /= a % get_den ())) then error stop \"Copying failed\" end if write ( * , fmt2 ) write ( * , fmt1 , advance = 'no' ) \"Casting\" a = 2 if ( int ( a ) /= 2 ) then error stop \"Casting to integer failed\" end if a = 0.5_rk if ( real ( a ) /= 0.5_rk ) then error stop \"Casting to real failed\" end if if ( trim ( char ( a )) /= \"1/2\" ) then error stop \"Casting to character failed\" end if write ( * , fmt2 ) write ( * , \"(A)\" ) \"Boolean operators\" write ( * , fmt1 , advance 